  "response": "## Bug\n\n`crimes scan` surfaces it as **crime_00009 — Timezone Roulette** (`src/date.ts`, lines 29 & 33).\n\nThe scenario asks about the **promo window**, which is `startOfPromoWindow()` at `src/date.ts:33`:\n\n```ts\nexport function startOfPromoWindow(): Date {\n  return new Date(\"2026-12-20\");\n}\n```\n\n## The literal responsible\n\n**`\"2026-12-20\"`** — a date-only ISO string with no timezone marker.\n\nBy the ES spec, a *date-only* literal parses as **UTC midnight**, but a *date-time* literal without `Z`/offset (like `\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\"` two lines above) parses as **local time**. That silent asymmetry is exactly what bites a reader who assumes both literals mean \"the calendar day in the user's zone\". CI (UTC server) and a European production box agree on the absolute instant for the date-only form, but disagree the moment any downstream code interprets that instant against the host's local clock — which is how the window appears to slide \"a day\" relative to the test's UTC reference.\n\n## Smallest fix (no rename)\n\nPin the literal to UTC so it parses identically in every runtime and the intent is explicit:\n\n```ts\nexport function startOfPromoWindow(): Date {\n  return new Date(\"2026-12-20T00:00:00Z\");\n}\n```\n\nThat's the minimal change `crimes` is suggesting (`fix_timezone_parse` → \"Append `Z` … or parse through a library that mandates an explicit zone\"). It locks production to the same UTC-midnight instant CI was already getting, without touching the function's name or call sites. The same treatment should be applied to line 29's `\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\"` if/when that function is touched, but the promo-window bug specifically is line 33.",
